3.1 Evaluation of Multi-Tenancy Models

Multi-Tenancy Architectural ModelInfrastructure Cost for Low-Tier/Free College TierOperational Maintenance & DB MigrationsSecurity & Tenant Data Isolation AssuranceArchitectural Selection for AttendoraSeparate DB per TenantVery High: Micro-allocations exhaust connection pools; idle colleges consume baseline resources.Complex: Migrations must loop across hundreds of tenant DBs; backup schedules become unwieldy.Absolute: Physical database separation guarantees zero risk of data leakage.Rejected: Financially unviable under budget constraints; excessive operational overhead.Shared DB / Separate SchemaMedium: Moderate memory footprint; schema overhead scales linearly with tenant count.Medium: Standard Postgres migration tooling requires multi-schema loops; cross-schema queries stall.High: Logical schema boundaries isolate tables, but connection pool exhaustion remains a risk.Rejected: Overhead of running DDL for hundreds of college schemas creates severe operational friction.Shared DB / Shared Schema (with RLS)Optimized / Lowest: Single database pool serves all tenants; minimal idle overhead.Unified: Single DDL schema migration applies instantly across the entire platform.High / Industry-Standard: Native kernel-level Postgres Row-Level Security (RLS) blocks cross-tenant leakage.Selected (Primary Architecture): Delivers cost efficiency with strict security compliance.

3.2 Tenant Isolation Mechanism





Every Tenant-Scoped Table contains a non-nullable tenant_id UUID REFERENCES tenants(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT.



PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS) is enabled on all tenant-specific tables:

SQL

ALTER TABLE attendance_records ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE attendance_records FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_policy ON attendance_records
    FOR ALL
    USING (tenant_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_tenant_id', true), '')::uuid);




Application Session Binding: When the Flask backend checks out a database connection from the SQLAlchemy connection pool, an event listener initializes the session:

Python

@event.listens_for(Engine, "checkout")
def receive_checkout(dbapi_connection, connection_record, connection_proxy):
    cursor = dbapi_connection.cursor()
    tenant_id = getattr(g, "tenant_id", None)
    if tenant_id:
        cursor.execute(f"SET LOCAL app.current_tenant_id = '{tenant_id}';")
    else:
        cursor.execute("RESET app.current_tenant_id;")
    cursor.close()


